A teacher at Swallowfield primary school in Woburn Sands, in Milton Keynes,=
 Darvill=E2=80=99s interest in walking football was sparked by supporting h=
er dad. She now coaches what is believed to be the first walking football t=
eam specifically dedicated to women with Parkinson=E2=80=99s,
https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IR9NJ=
KsxYgqJheqAQJ2aJHuBTAiGV7txo6Oxv3h9idbuYYmh-mBOFpltiw5VVDrjPLX__xZiUV7oebBg=
Vj8HP0qz3WH3gts0aKVmgcH8lnm5luaNqywvM5s-rjyUGBqAOaj3APg3I8ZjlVYoLIYKBWe5ZyC=
rXwkChf23pdUXjTbaYrlv80gWEnKOo0jCOmBdiE6wagyVv6TqtqBsGicNNIo19JvOo85xE26fHL=
uMGzxWJ/4u1/Yp7UpExMT9yKLxwRbX8h2A/t3/h001.hx2yTOzfjzUbRRfthkfAHyOYxAUaHlIo=
g0bADNjp8t0
the Parkinson=E2=80=99s Pioneers
, who train in Solihull and have seen a tenfold increase in their membershi=
p in just over three years. That led to her appointment as the coach of the=
 first England women=E2=80=99s national side.

There are reportedly about 166,000 people in the UK living with Parkinson=
=E2=80=99s disease. It is a progressive brain condition for which there is =
no cure, but known treatments
https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IR7eq=
a6bER7lgw_49fRrzXG-ToPCjIFb6y_j74XWIS48j8Sqp7AADQP17e1E2Z7tQG9kxMFRXcJ0cqW6=
Bn1_hVnMCR17ZkuZNFRWHk9FrZkaox_4BRxioJMcaeENqxextpayKvgcglnMpJrq6Z-j4GglhnY=
HI5Xj9NvArnMvwJIzuj06-no0gjqiFv-WLs_-N1kLacGLcrOYkcZDw0mQC75au4FInzAHWHzvBh=
n_aeZYx/4u1/Yp7UpExMT9yKLxwRbX8h2A/t4/h001.aUAadlN37dgCK0w89mpd2ddEFXfGngle=
V2Qxbw6HldA
can help reduce the severity of the main symptoms
. Darvill, who says exercise =E2=80=9Chelped slow down the progression of t=
he disease=E2=80=9D for her dad, recalled: =E2=80=9CWhen he was first diagn=
osed, it hits you hard. But football genuinely saved his life.

=E2=80=9CAt first, he didn=E2=80=99t want to leave the house. He used to ha=
ve a season ticket at Tottenham [but] he gave that up. He didn=E2=80=99t te=
ll any of his friends. He just stopped doing everything. He really struggle=
d with life in general, [even] getting out of bed. And then we found this f=
ootball session. His team=E2=80=99s called
https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IR7eq=
a6bER7lgw_49fRrzXG-ToPCjIFb6y_j74XWIS48j8Sqp7AADQP17e1E2Z7tQG9kxMFRXcJ0cqW6=
Bn1_hVnMCR17ZkuZNFRWHk9FrZkaox_4BRxioJMcaeENqxextpayKvgcglnMpJrq6Z-j4GglhnY=
HI5Xj9NvArnMvwJIzuj06-no0gjqiFv-WLs_-N1kLacGLcrOYkcZDw0mQC75au4FInzAHWHzvBh=
n_aeZYx/4u1/Yp7UpExMT9yKLxwRbX8h2A/t5/h001.pbhuyWhvBolIKlAGDOpxwTQSnDvl547y=
xgmRqQfB-9A
Fighting Fit Football
[based at Watford=E2=80=99s training ground]. I took him along one week and=
 he=E2=80=99s never looked back since.

=E2=80=9CSo, just to be able to give back a little bit, I guess that=E2=80=
=99s why I still do it: to help others who get diagnosed, to show them that=
 there is still a life after being diagnosed with the disease and you can m=
eet lots of incredible people, and still try to do things as normally as po=
ssible.=E2=80=9D


Gemma Darvill=E2=80=99s first coaching role was with the Parkinson=E2=80=99=
s Pioneers, a team she helped set up in 2023. Photograph: Handout


Darvill, who has played football since she was 10, was spotted cheering for=
 her dad on the sidelines of a tournament at St George=E2=80=99s Park and w=
as promptly asked by Annie Booth, the founder of the Parkinson=E2=80=99s Pi=
oneers, if she would be interested in helping set up the team. To Darvill, =
helping out felt natural: =E2=80=9CI=E2=80=99ve always coached football as =
well as played, and I was a teacher, so I used to run football camps for ki=
ds in the school holidays.=E2=80=9D

Booth and Darvill have seen the Parkinson=E2=80=99s Pioneers blossom into a=
 flourishing community, explaining: =E2=80=9CI remember our very first trai=
ning session, it was only three of us. We now have over 40 ladies coming to=
 the sessions. It=E2=80=99s incredible.

=E2=80=9CThey use it as a social network as well. So after the sessions, ev=
eryone stays for a bit of food, a conversation, and they can just discuss l=
ike their symptoms, how they=E2=80=99re coping. And we=E2=80=99re family no=
w, really, if I=E2=80=99m honest. Everyone just helps each other out.

=E2=80=9CWe train at Solihull purely because the ladies come from all over =
the country. We have ladies from Manchester, we have myself coming from Mil=
ton Keynes, we have people in Devon.

=E2=80=9CSo it=E2=80=99s the most central location that we could find to tr=
y to make it as fair as possible for everyone travelling. So we try to meet=
 up once a month and then we enter the tournaments as well.=E2=80=9D

As well as prohibiting running, walking football includes several different=
 rules to the mainstream game. There is no offside rule, no heading, no thr=
ow-ins and it is a non-contact game. Darvill says: =E2=80=9CI=E2=80=99d say=
 the tactics are the same [in general]. But you can=E2=80=99t tackle from t=
he side or from behind in walking football =E2=80=93 it has to be face-on.


Darvill=E2=80=99s first involvement with walking football for people with P=
arkinson=E2=80=99s came through helping her father after his diagnosis. Pho=
tograph: Handout


=E2=80=9CEveryone with Parkinson=E2=80=99s has a very different level of th=
e condition. The walking element just helps keep them all safe. We have a b=
rilliant physio on board as well who is specifically a Parkinson=E2=80=99s =
physio, which is brilliant, and she helps us a lot with the warm-ups and th=
e cool-downs and just makes sure that ladies are getting what they need. It=
 makes their brain think about something else. [For example] I=E2=80=99ve g=
ot one lady who can=E2=80=99t walk in a straight line but if you put a spor=
ts ladder down in front of her, she can walk through it. It=E2=80=99s reall=
y interesting.=E2=80=9D

The growth of the Pioneers meant that Darvill was the natural pick when the=
 Walking Football Association (WFA) was launching the first England women=
=E2=80=99s Parkinson=E2=80=99s team with trials last year. Then, in August =
this year, the WFA and the Football Association
https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.wtfWA9uPAn8i7Lb0U_hU2v9n=
ZlmCV7oJ1Y4sUxPOpSmH6JCPKOcMYNzB2wr12FLzLjUdIfwLs0xvzbS55OgMhsAhkGiwgsGUODu=
1teDeTTm6UDGwSJkRbsOkb0RvvogGYXC4h8vtSUmjngD9d3yyzBRFiAa_4qpWhthwSn65_azlkM=
4AB6f8_E2fymekU-7PoG7Kg9FWOKG_chTH6szWWdWYH_BVsl6OExNJjrrxVnahPTsyTH3B9i2_T=
xbXKuSEaSGwykGSZtpkOuHOEghgpOlKZr_GjgjtL_28GABpC7w/4u1/Yp7UpExMT9yKLxwRbX8h=
2A/t6/h001.e8KsF2-S6IdE5D_rGm_QC9GuA6sBCpnyk2iT0FKCWAI
announced a new partnership
that will install the FA as walking football=E2=80=99s governing body in En=
gland with the WFA now the delivery partner for the game. The collaboration=
 was partly aimed at benefits such as being able to streamline the registra=
tion of referees and agree on a unified set of rules. Darvill adds: =E2=80=
=9CThat=E2=80=99s a positive, going forward, that they=E2=80=99re coming to=
gether a little bit more.=E2=80=9D

Darvill=E2=80=99s England team play six-a-side and the players range in age=
 from 36 to 66. They have held training sessions in Watford, Droitwich and =
Bedford so far, but are about to cross the Atlantic to join the England men=
=E2=80=99s Parkinson=E2=80=99s team on a landmark trip to Canada for an int=
ernational showcase of the benefits of walking football and a series of inv=
itational friendlies.

The team travel on Saturday for one week in Toronto. The teams have been cr=
owdfunding to try to help cover the course of the trip. Darvill=E2=80=99s
